From 474af02277d6ff034509b329b5dfb8f05191946b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Eden (Doris) build" <doris@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 00:07:39 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 43/46] Eden (Doris): GPU drawings survive game writes next to
 them; copy-backs cover 512 KiB; barriers only where needed; report names
 textures and shows the clock

- Eden marks every texture on a page the game's code writes to as CPU-written and uploads it
  again from guest memory, which still holds what was there before the GPU drew into it (build
  18: up to 400 such uploads every 10 s in TotK). On an NCE write fault, a GPU-drawn texture's
  bytes on that page are kept as they were before the write and the page is released; at the
  texture's next use (or the end of the frame) they are compared: if the game changed them the
  texture is uploaded again as in Eden, otherwise what the GPU drew stays. Also when the buffer
  cache copies GPU-written bytes back first.
- Copying back GPU-written buffer bytes before a game write (Eden #4490/#4531) waits for the GPU
  to finish everything queued, about 20 ms while a frame is in flight, and TotK then writes the
  next pages of the same buffer (bursts of 10 to 13 waits). The copy back now covers the
  surrounding 512 KiB. BufferCache::DownloadMemory limits each buffer's download to the part of
  the range inside it.
- GPU barriers default 3: a wait-for-idle with no draw since the previous one that wrote storage
  buffers or images (or used transform feedback) records nothing and keeps the render pass open
  (build 18: 113 of 130 per frame). 1 keeps a barrier at every wait-for-idle.
- Report: the device's clock time on every 10-second window, and the textures (size, format)
  that were uploaded again over GPU-drawn contents, had their bytes changed by the game, or were
  removed with GPU-drawn contents lost.

